Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 16:39:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 167/175] docs: the film grain spec is based on dev, not on the
 scene-referred tree

The grain work was rebased onto dev@28a492c1 by the user's decision, so the spec's base
line and its one scene-referred claim in section 2 no longer described the tree it belongs
to. On dev nothing forces the stock grain amount to 0 except the block's own
Grain_User_Active, which is what the composition pass now does.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QQ89XhSX1rRM69nEDRFats
---
 Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/FilmGrainSpec.md | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/FilmGrainSpec.md b/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/FilmGrainSpec.md
index 5428993e..38e74985 100644
--- a/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/FilmGrainSpec.md
+++ b/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/FilmGrainSpec.md
@@ -1,7 +1,8 @@
 # Film Grain — specification
 
 Status: approved for implementation 2026-09-05 (branch `grain`, worktree `CRYENGINE_Grain`, based on
-`scene@d8567f37`). Author: Fable. Implementer: Opus, one stage per agent. Working notes (outside git):
+`dev@28a492c1`, the stock pipeline; rebased off `scene` on 2026-09-05 by the user's decision).
+Author: Fable. Implementer: Opus, one stage per agent. Working notes (outside git):
 `scene-notes/grain/` — `01-requirements.md` (R-numbers), `PROPOSALS.md` (design space, §0 shared
 skeleton), `research/01-ce-native-grain.md` (stock implementation, every claim with file:line),
 `decisions/01-generator-and-placement.md`.
@@ -31,8 +32,9 @@ Two generator families, one skeleton:
   same slot, same order relative to sharpen / overlays. Pre-ODT placement is the future upgrade once TAA
   moves in front of the tonemap; the interface in §4 does not change then (R1.6).
 - The stock `ApplyFilmGrain` (`PostAA.cfx:905-924`) and its amount path (`PostAA.cpp:408-412`,
-  `HDRParams.w`) stay exactly as they are for the inactive case. Under the scene-referred switch the stock
-  amount is already forced to 0 (`PostAA.cpp:427`).
+  `HDRParams.w`) stay exactly as they are for the inactive case. On the `dev` base there is no
+  scene-referred switch, so the block's own `Grain_User_Active` is the only thing that forces the
+  stock amount to 0.
 - Source / destination at that point: `$DisplayTargetDst` R10G10B10A2 UNORM → swap-chain R8G8B8A8 UNORM
   (research §2.3). The write is 8-bit: grain σ must stay above ~1 LSB where it is meant to be visible (R8.4).
 
